# The basket — a confirmation taken both ways, and a line dragged from one list to another.
#
# **`within` is load-bearing here and is not decoration.** There are three `Remove` buttons on the
# order page, so `click button "Remove"` is an ambiguous locator and tflw refuses it rather than
# picking one — the same refusal `signin.tflw` records for `expect text "Sign in"`. Scoping to the
# list names one of the three.

# **The answer is armed BEFORE the question is asked, and the first draft had it the other way
# round.** A native `confirm()` blocks the page until something answers it, so there is no moment
# between the click and the dialog in which a test could decide — `accept dialog` says what to do
# with the *next* one. tflw says so when you get it wrong, which is how this file was corrected:
# `warning[TF079]: dismiss dialog armed the next native dialog and no dialog was raised before the
# test ended — the arming did nothing`. That warning is on a test that PASSED, which is the point
# of it: the assertion after a mis-ordered arm is true for a reason the author did not write.

@checkout @functional
test "removing a saved item asks first, and yes means yes"
  open "/order"
  accept dialog
  within list "Saved for later"
    click button "Remove"
  expect dialog message equals "Remove Filter coffee, 1kg from your saved items?"
  expect css "#saved-line" is hidden

@checkout @functional
test "…and no leaves the item where it was"
  open "/order"
  dismiss dialog
  within list "Saved for later"
    click button "Remove"
  expect text "Kept in your saved items" is visible

@checkout @functional
test "a saved item can be dragged into the basket"
  open "/order"
  drag css "#saved-line" to css "#basket"
  expect text "Moved Filter coffee, 1kg to your basket" is visible
